items = {"foo": "The Foo Wrestlers"}


@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(item_id: str):
    if item_id not in items:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Item not found")
    return {"item": items[item_id]}

The resulting response

If the client requests http://example.com/items/foo (an item_id "foo" ), that client will receive an HTTP status code of 200, and a JSON response of:

{
  "item": "The Foo Wrestlers"
}

But if the client requests http://example.com/items

you go to the browser at http://127.0.0.1:8000/items/foo , you will see a nice HTTP error of:

{
  "detail": [
    {
      "type": "int_parsing",
      "loc": [
        "path",
        "item_id"
      ],
      "msg": "Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer",
      "input": "foo"
    }
  ]
}

because the path parameter item_id had a value of "foo" , which is not an int .

The same error would appear if you provided a float instead of an int , as in: http://127.0.0.1

